<h2>The contracts and the collision</h2>
<p>The petitioner held a civil works contract of November 2024, valued at over Rs. 86 crore, for the Pareng Hydro Electric Project. Two bank guarantees secured the mobilisation advance. A separate framework agreement of January 2025 governed the supply of cement and steel. When relations deteriorated, a legal notice of June 2026 demanded roughly Rs. 3.71 crore under the framework agreement — and the petitioner, anticipating encashment of the guarantees, moved the High Court under Section 9, contending that guarantees furnished under one contract were being weaponised for claims under another. It also invoked the arbitration clause in the alternative.</p>
<p>The respondent's answer was orthodox: the guarantees belonged to the civil works contract alone, were in substance unconditional given their non-obstante language, and the petitioner had suppressed correspondence recording its defaults.</p>

<h2>How the court reasoned</h2>
<div class="flow">
<div class="fstep"><strong>Trace the invocation.</strong> Reading the notices, the court held the invocation was purely under the civil works contract, not the framework agreement; the June demand notice was a separate thread.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Characterise the guarantee.</strong> The guarantees were partly conditional. The first condition — commencement of works — was met; on the second, the correspondence disclosed prima facie default. The conditions being satisfied at this threshold, the bank was bound to honour its commitment.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Apply the separation principle.</strong> Following the Supreme Court's decision in NHAI v. Ganga Enterprises, the guarantee is a contract separate from the underlying agreement; the court looks to the guarantee's own terms, not the merits of the construction dispute. The recent Kalpataru ruling was distinguished as a case about an unconditional guarantee.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Leave merits to arbitration.</strong> Whether the invocation was ultimately justified in account terms is for the arbitral tribunal; Section 9 is not a preview of the award.</div>
</div>
<div class="note">
<p>The court closed with a disclosure warning: the petitioner had not placed material correspondence on record, and Section 9 petitioners must include all material documents. The petition was dismissed without costs and interim protection vacated.</p>
</div>

<h2>The doctrine, compressed</h2>
<p>Bank guarantee law balances two interests. Commerce requires that guarantees function as cash — a beneficiary who bargained for an on-demand instrument must not be forced to litigate before payment. Equity intervenes only where the invocation is itself vitiated: established fraud known to the bank, irretrievable injustice, or a conditional guarantee whose triggering conditions are unmet. The third exception is the only one that regularly rewards close drafting analysis, and this judgment shows its mechanics: the court reads the guarantee's conditions, checks them prima facie against the record, and stops there. What it will not do is convert the enquiry into an audit of the underlying contract.</p>
<p>The disclosure holding deserves equal attention. Interim relief moves fast and often ex parte; the jurisdiction survives on candour. A Section 9 petition that files the contract but omits the default notices invites exactly the response delivered here.</p>

<h2>Practical pointers</h2>
<div class="check">
<p>Before furnishing a guarantee, negotiate its conditions consciously: every condition is a potential ground to resist invocation, and every non-obstante clause erodes one.</p>
<p>When resisting encashment, plead within the recognised exceptions and tie the argument to the guarantee's text; underlying-contract grievances belong in arbitration.</p>
<p>File the complete correspondence, favourable and otherwise; suppression converts a weak petition into a dismissed one.</p>
<p>Beneficiaries should invoke strictly in the guarantee's language and under the correct contract — a mismatched invocation is the one self-inflicted wound this field still sees.</p>
</div>
